AVM: HVF puts a slot back to its own permissions when dirty tracking stops,
so the UEFI variable flash stays read-only after a savevm

accel/hvf/hvf-accel-ops.c's hvf_set_dirty_tracking made every slot
read-write when tracking stopped. Saving a running machine's memory (a
restore point taken while it runs, AVM's Save, any migration) starts and
stops tracking on every slot, including the UEFI variable store's
pflash, a ROM device in romd mode that hvf_set_phys_mem maps read-only
so the guest's writes trap to the CFI command handler. Made writable,
the firmware's commands went into the flash's memory instead: at the
next boot the firmware wrote the read-status command (0x70) and read
back its own 0x70, never the ready bit, and Windows Boot Manager waited
on a black screen forever (2026-10-31: two restore points taken by the
before-updates check, then reproduced with QEMU alone; a register sync
alone, the in-kernel GIC off and the TPM off all made no difference,
and a paused save of the device state without the disk still hung).
Stopping now restores what hvf_set_phys_mem gave the slot, and a
section with no slot (a ROM device out of romd mode) is skipped instead
of dereferenced.

Applied after UTM's own patches/qemu-10.0.12-utm.patch, which doesn't
touch this function.

--- a/accel/hvf/hvf-accel-ops.c
+++ b/accel/hvf/hvf-accel-ops.c
@@ -305,10 +305,15 @@
 static void hvf_set_dirty_tracking(MemoryRegionSection *section, bool on)
 {
     hvf_slot *slot;
+    MemoryRegion *area = section->mr;
 
     slot = hvf_find_overlap_slot(
             section->offset_within_address_space,
             int128_get64(section->size));
+    /* AVM: a ROM device out of romd mode has no slot; nothing to track */
+    if (!slot) {
+        return;
+    }
 
     /* protect region against writes; begin tracking it */
     if (on) {
@@ -318,8 +323,20 @@
     /* stop tracking region*/
     } else {
         slot->flags &= ~HVF_SLOT_LOG;
-        hv_vm_protect((uintptr_t)slot->start, (size_t)slot->size,
-                      HV_MEMORY_READ | HV_MEMORY_WRITE | HV_MEMORY_EXEC);
+        /*
+         * AVM: back to the permissions hvf_set_phys_mem gave the slot, not
+         * always writable. A ROM device in romd mode (the UEFI variable
+         * flash) must stay read-only, so the guest's writes trap to the
+         * device's command handler; made writable after a migration or a
+         * savevm, the firmware's commands landed in its memory and it
+         * waited forever for a status that never came.
+         */
+        hv_memory_flags_t flags = HV_MEMORY_READ | HV_MEMORY_EXEC;
+        if (!area->readonly &&
+            !(!memory_region_is_ram(area) && memory_region_is_romd(area))) {
+            flags |= HV_MEMORY_WRITE;
+        }
+        hv_vm_protect((uintptr_t)slot->start, (size_t)slot->size, flags);
     }
 }
 
